# The 24-cell, the unique self-dual regular polytope in four dimensions: twenty-four vertices, ninety-six edges, ninety-
# six faces, twenty-four octahedral cells. Its vertices are the D4 roots, the 4-vectors with exactly two nonzero slots,
# each plus or minus one. This file CONSTRUCTS that vertex set as a type you compute with: a vertex is an axis-pair with a
# sign on each, the antipode (negate both signs) is a real function, and the structure is proved, the antipode an
# involution with no fixed point, so the twenty-four vertices pair into the twelve lines the law acts on, and the sign-
# choices give distinct vertices. These are the twenty-four directions a tone may step. Four independent constructions
# (D4 roots, the two-nonzero ternary shell, the unit Hurwitz quaternions, the 4D kissing configuration) meet on these
# same points, which is how the figure is known to be forced.

# A sign on a nonzero coordinate.
form sign
  case neg
  case pos

# Which two of the four axes (w, x, y, z) are nonzero: the six unordered pairs.
form duo
  case wx
  case wy
  case wz
  case xy
  case xz
  case yz

# A vertex of the 24-cell: a choice of two nonzero axes and a sign on each.
form vertex
  case at
    link axes, like duo
    link sign-one, like sign
    link sign-two, like sign

# TEMPLATE: one entry of a single-valued map on signs, `from` sent to `to`. Replaces every
# `case .. / send back / make ..` block with one line per entry.
tree map-sign
  take from
  take to

  hook fuse
    case {from}
      send back
        make {to}

# Flip a sign.
task flip-sign
  take s, like sign
  like sign
  fork case, read s
    fuse map-sign, neg, pos
    fuse map-sign, pos, neg

# The antipode of a vertex: the diametrically opposite point, both signs flipped, the other end of its line.
task antipode
  take v, like vertex
  like vertex
  fork case, read v
    case at
      link axes
      link sign-one
      link sign-two
      send back
        make at
          bind axes
            read axes
          bind sign-one
            call flip-sign
              read sign-one
          bind sign-two
            call flip-sign
              read sign-two

# A truth value, to test vertex equality.
form flag
  case yes
  case no

task same-sign
  take a, like sign
  take b, like sign
  like flag
  fork case, read a
    case neg
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-sign, neg, yes
        fuse map-sign, pos, no
    case pos
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-sign, neg, no
        fuse map-sign, pos, yes

task and-flag
  take a, like flag
  take b, like flag
  like flag
  fork case, read a
    case yes
      send back
        read b
    case no
      send back
        make no

# Two vertices are the same when their axis-pair and both signs agree. (Same axes assumed; the law acts within a line.)
task same-vertex
  take a, like vertex
  take b, like vertex
  like flag
  fork case, read a
    case at
      link axes-a
      link s1
      link s2
      fork case, read b
        case at
          link axes-b
          link t1
          link t2
          send back
            call and-flag
              call same-sign
                read s1
                read t1
              call same-sign
                read s2
                read t2

# The ANTIPODE is an INVOLUTION: antipode (antipode v) = v, for every vertex. The opposite of the opposite is the start.
rule antipode-is-an-involution
  seat v, like vertex
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call antipode
        call antipode
          read v
      read v
  fold v

# A sign and its flip always differ: same-sign s (flip-sign s) = no. The seed of the antipode having no fixed point.
rule flip-sign-differs
  seat s, like sign
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call same-sign
        read s
        call flip-sign
          read s
      make no
  fold s

# The antipode is FIXED-POINT-FREE: no vertex equals its own antipode, same-vertex v (antipode v) = no. Because the
# antipode flips both signs and a flipped sign always differs. This is what makes the twenty-four vertices pair into
# twelve genuine lines (antipodal pairs), with none its own partner.
rule antipode-has-no-fixed-point
  seat v, like vertex
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call same-vertex
        read v
        call antipode
          read v
      make no
  fold v

# The count is STRUCTURAL, not asserted. The vertex type IS a choice of axis-pair (six) with a sign on each (two by two),
# so the inhabitants of `vertex` ARE the twenty-four vertices by construction; no `24 = 6 * 4` assertion is needed. And
# they pair into twelve antipodal lines because `antipode-has-no-fixed-point` above PROVES the antipode is a fixed-point-
# free involution: pairing each vertex with its distinct opposite partitions the twenty-four into twelve, the real content
# that `24 = 2 * 12` only gestured at. The same twenty-four points, as unit quaternions, form the binary tetrahedral GROUP
# closed under the multiplication proved in `group/quaternion`. So the 24-cell is a constructed type with a computable
# antipode and a group law, not a number.

# DISTINCTNESS: the sign-choices of an axis-pair give DIFFERENT vertices, so the construction has no collisions. A vertex
# and the one with its first sign flipped are distinct, shown by refutation over the sign.
rule sign-flip-gives-distinct-vertex
  seat a, like duo
  seat s, like sign
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call same-vertex
        make at
          bind axes
            read a
          bind sign-one
            read s
          bind sign-two
            make pos
        make at
          bind axes
            read a
          bind sign-one
            call flip-sign
              read s
          bind sign-two
            make pos
      make no
  fold s
